#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124247 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124443 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3991a54-342b-5cd0-87e2-606146240555" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient of a Function of Two Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "526b792b-9952-5dae-bcd6-15564c0e013f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3131" :question/problem "Find $\\nabla f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,\\pi)$ given that $f(x,y) = (x - y)\\cos y.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x-y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x-y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-(x-y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\text{ }i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\text{ }i-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+(x-y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the gradient at the specified point, we substitute $(x,y)= (1,\\pi)$ into the above, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(1,\\pi ) & ={f}_{x}(1,\\pi )\\text{ }i+{f}_{y}(1,\\pi )\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\text{ }i-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +(1-\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124248 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124444 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe2c7ce2-f053-57af-9f4d-54bd3a41648a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient of a Function of Three Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "bfade25e-065a-5c66-a554-9ee4e9559a55" :question/math-academy-id "e-3132" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\nabla g(x,y,z)$   at $(2,-1,3)$ if $g(x,y,z) = x^2 + xyz.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+xyz) \\\\  & =2x+yz \\\\ {g}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+xyz) \\\\  & =0+xz \\\\  & =xz \\\\ {g}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+xyz) \\\\  & =0+xy \\\\  & =xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & ={g}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }i+{g}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{g}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(2x+yz)\\text{ }i+(xz)\\text{ }j+(xy)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇g(2,-1,3) & =(2(2)+(-1)(3))\\text{ }i+2(3)\\text{ }j+2(-1)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =1\\text{ }i+6\\text{ }j-2\\text{ }k \\\\  & =i+6\\text{ }j-2\\text{ }k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124249 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124445 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8d807a6-7896-5045-a14a-908b58831340" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points Where a Gradient Vanishes"}] :question/id #uuid "b2549d7b-07c2-5f2f-89a3-2cccecf2ba22" :question/math-academy-id "e-3133" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)= (y-4)\\ln (x-2),$ find the points $(x, y)$ where $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((y-4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{y-4}{x-2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((y-4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\dfrac {y-4}{x-2} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x-2) \\, \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {y-4}{x-2} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x-2) \\, \\mathbf j = 0 \\  \\mathbf i + 0\\ \\mathbf j\n$$\n\nBy equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{i}$ on both sides of the equation (and similarly with $\\mathbf{j}$), we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-4}{x-2}=0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y-4=0,$ which means $y=4.$  From the second equation, we obtain $\\ln(x-2)=0,$ which means that $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(x,y) =\\mathbf  0$ at the point $(3,4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124251 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124447 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67cfec73-130c-5b68-a2e0-5f12837bfc81" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient Using the Properties of the Gradient"}] :question/id #uuid "67e9bcb5-4bf2-56b8-8d82-95355841eaaa" :question/math-academy-id "e-3150" :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y) g(x,y)$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =xy,\\, &  & ∇f(x,y)=y\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j, &  &  \\\\ g(x,y) & =\\sqrt{xy},\\, &  & ∇g(x,y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{xy}}{2x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{\\sqrt{xy}}{2y}\\text{ }j. &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\nabla [fg] = g \\, \\nabla\\! f  + f \\, \\nabla\\! g.\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule shown above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y)g(x,y)] & =g(x,y)∇f(x,y)+f(x,y)∇g(x,y) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{xy}(y\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j)+xy(\\frac{\\sqrt{xy}}{2x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{\\sqrt{xy}}{2y}\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{xy}(y\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j)+\\frac{\\sqrt{xy}}{2}(y\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{xy}}{2}(y\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437017 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124443 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3991a54-342b-5cd0-87e2-606146240555" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient of a Function of Two Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15a96f20-3d44-594c-b78c-5063ef876b55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+e\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436981} {:answer/id #uuid "586b4ce1-ff41-581f-8671-763767e3f3aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436985} {:answer/id #uuid "e58c9d0d-900d-5547-a140-989e42e2459e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437002} {:answer/id #uuid "f50ec89d-eacd-5a88-8f97-5ca1603aa25f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e\\,i+e\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437006} {:answer/id #uuid "fbc7838c-e6a9-592d-8e12-48498680e92d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15a96f20-3d44-594c-b78c-5063ef876b55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+e\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436981} :answer-field/id #uuid "51d09cbe-76cd-5f94-9148-15ee4e3c70f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437010}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "51a75799-7672-5c71-a422-88f2c2c1de7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-127652" :question/problem "Evaluate $∇f(x,y)$ at $(e,1)$ if $f(x,y)={x}^{y}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{y}) \\\\  & =y{x}^{y-1} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{y}) \\\\  & ={x}^{y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =y{x}^{y-1}\\text{ }i+{x}^{y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\text{ }j, \\\\ ∇f(e,1) & =1(e)^{1-1}\\,i+{e}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡e\\,j \\\\  & =i+e\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437016 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124444 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe2c7ce2-f053-57af-9f4d-54bd3a41648a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient of a Function of Three Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b7ebbbc-7508-52dc-91d2-56b705b7eccc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-12,0,8⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436986} {:answer/id #uuid "763a0475-ce2e-53a8-aa2d-9466219586f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨12,8,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436989} {:answer/id #uuid "bd9e8470-5c37-54ae-b712-f0a64e63b2e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,8,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436995} {:answer/id #uuid "c0ae3e99-1ea5-5fa4-a1e9-7139e200dfcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,-4,8⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436996} {:answer/id #uuid "e7d9044c-103d-5c75-a1cc-b44786993ed3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-4,0,8⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd9e8470-5c37-54ae-b712-f0a64e63b2e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,8,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436995} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe5a3950-f690-5d04-879c-231b6f838613" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3edc7251-4f74-5afa-bcee-22e839f081a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-127946" :question/problem "Evaluate $∇h(x,y,z)$ at $(1,-2,2)$ if $h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{y}^{2}+2yz.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{y}^{2}+2yz) \\\\  & =2x{z}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{y}^{2}+2yz) \\\\  & =2z-2xy \\\\ {h}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{y}^{2}+2yz) \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}z+2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇h(x,y,z) & =⟨{h}_{x}(x,y,z),\\,{h}_{y}(x,y,z),\\,{h}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2x{z}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,2z-2xy,\\,2{x}^{2}z+2y⟩ \\\\ ∇h(1,-2,2) & =⟨2(1)(2)^{2}-(-2)^{2},\\,-2(1)(-2)+2(2),\\,2(1)^{2}(2)+2(-2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8-4,4+4,4-4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,8,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437020 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124445 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8d807a6-7896-5045-a14a-908b58831340" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points Where a Gradient Vanishes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e36d0b8-dad3-53f0-905d-1b73b0cfb007" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,-1,-1),$ $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436982} {:answer/id #uuid "3eb3ce4f-3615-5638-9cca-7d5f9a7f4065" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,-1,-1),$ $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436983} {:answer/id #uuid "4a50ec3f-8ef4-53d6-b614-888ffb598364" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,-1,-1)$ and $(1,1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436984} {:answer/id #uuid "b421e42f-f55a-5ffe-a533-367af3998505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,-1,-1)$ and $(-1,-1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436993} {:answer/id #uuid "ca48ce3d-8118-5a85-8d40-9f54dccebe39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,-1,-1)$ and $(0,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3eb3ce4f-3615-5638-9cca-7d5f9a7f4065" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,-1,-1),$ $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436983} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9f85d81-d1f0-5077-ba52-dc9b36b72fa3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437014}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c3503a39-2405-5c8f-9ad7-2d39b5c187d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-128513" :question/problem "Given $h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+4yz-2{z}^{2}-2{y}^{2}x.$ Find the points $(x,y,z)$ where $∇h(x,y,z)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+4yz-2{z}^{2}-2{y}^{2}x) \\\\  & =2x-2{y}^{2} \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+4yz-2{z}^{2}-2{y}^{2}x) \\\\  & =4z-4yx \\\\ {h}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+4yz-2{z}^{2}-2{y}^{2}x) \\\\  & =4y-4z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇h(x,y,z)=2(x-{y}^{2})\\,i+4(z-yx)\\,j+4(y-z)\\,k.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇f(x,y,z)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x-{y}^{2})\\,i+4(z-yx)\\,j+4(y-z)\\,k & =0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j+0\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$, $j$, and $k$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-{y}^{2}=0, \\\\ z-yx=0, \\\\ y-z=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first equation gives $x={y}^{2}.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain $z={y}^{3}.$ Substituting $z={y}^{3}$ into the third equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ y(1-{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\,\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $y=-1,$ we get $x=(-1)^{2}=1$ and $z=(-1)^{3}=-1.$\n\n- For $y=0,$ we obtain $x={0}^{2}=0$ and $z={0}^{3}=0.$\n\n- For $y=1,$ gives $x={1}^{2}=1$ and $z={1}^{3}=1.$\n\nFinally, $∇h(x,y,z)=0$ at three points: $(1,-1,-1),$ $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,1,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125020 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124444 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe2c7ce2-f053-57af-9f4d-54bd3a41648a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient of a Function of Three Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35a99596-2194-56d8-b34a-8cf33e24d509" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{xyz}(x\\text{ }i+y\\text{ }j+z\\text{ }k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321558} {:answer/id #uuid "6fa3bf93-c16d-5b28-921e-41079fe50f98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xyz}{2}(\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{z}\\text{ }k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322014} {:answer/id #uuid "bdfd3cdb-b6de-5b57-8bc2-55f6cfdc0358" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2}(\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{z}\\text{ }k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322622} {:answer/id #uuid "ce59c82e-5d51-5485-be48-7fb4d8c385b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2}(x\\text{ }i+y\\text{ }j+z\\text{ }k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322758} {:answer/id #uuid "def79f1e-967d-536d-901e-8b6506e4e5d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{xyz}(\\frac{1}{yz}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{xz}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{xz}\\text{ }k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322890}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdfd3cdb-b6de-5b57-8bc2-55f6cfdc0358" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2}(\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{z}\\text{ }k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322622} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1682cef-8ea8-5e2a-8b48-c4bffce5ce2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323514}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4903eb08-1095-52c0-bd3a-a8d7c39455f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-36918" :question/problem "Given $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{xyz}$, find $∇f(x,y,z).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{xyz}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{xyz}}\\cdot (yz) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2x} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{xyz}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{xyz}}\\cdot (xz) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2y} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{xyz}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{xyz}}\\cdot (xy) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2y}\\text{ }j+\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2z}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2}(\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{z}\\text{ }k)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335800 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124447 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67cfec73-130c-5b68-a2e0-5f12837bfc81" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient Using the Properties of the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10ef7585-f490-52ce-84d4-c14afbd71f8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+2)\\text{ }i+x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+2)\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333143} {:answer/id #uuid "21b12eb3-67b9-5f76-80f9-150884fc0d5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+1)\\text{ }i+x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+1)\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333290} {:answer/id #uuid "3ea7c594-0a56-5e0b-a1fd-7fd5413db99e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }i-y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333580} {:answer/id #uuid "f9a4ec80-da82-5689-8ecb-e79e98294b6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }i+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335176} {:answer/id #uuid "fe69f0a9-435b-5c17-9a63-9c54c8b0d0ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }i+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335228}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21b12eb3-67b9-5f76-80f9-150884fc0d5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+1)\\text{ }i+x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+1)\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333290} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8f1d221-bfab-57b4-bd66-7eea20107505" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "838d032d-5600-5cb9-9af4-36cc70864ee1" :question/math-academy-id "q-65131" :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y)g(x,y),$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =xy,\\, &  & ∇f(x,y)=y\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j, &  &  \\\\ g(x,y) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy),\\, &  & ∇g(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j. &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ is given by:\n\n$$\n∇[fg]=g\\,∇\\,f+f\\,∇\\,g\n$$\n\nSo, using this product rule we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y)g(x,y)] & =g(x,y)∇f(x,y)+f(x,y)∇g(x,y) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))(y\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j)+xy(\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }i+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }j+y\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j \\\\  & =[y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }i+y\\text{ }i]+[x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }j+x\\text{ }j] \\\\  & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+1)\\text{ }i+x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+1)\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437019 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124443 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3991a54-342b-5cd0-87e2-606146240555" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient of a Function of Two Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04308c81-1719-5c33-8d40-ebccd5767820" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436980} {:answer/id #uuid "8e9a1c99-2d0e-5085-aa56-1bc7935a41a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,\\,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436990} {:answer/id #uuid "b75c28ff-2fd2-5cb7-a89b-d7658bb7354e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436994} {:answer/id #uuid "d8f83b16-7c14-555d-b3bd-510c637f070d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437000} {:answer/id #uuid "e717d850-f7b3-516d-8057-a5abe63141f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,\\,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437003}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b75c28ff-2fd2-5cb7-a89b-d7658bb7354e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436994} :answer-field/id #uuid "798ed032-e7e1-5b90-a986-cd5c9792165f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bace43f6-9821-5763-9bf8-9ca1cf7da037" :question/math-academy-id "q-71353" :question/problem "Evaluate $∇h(x,y)$ at $(1,-1)$ if $h(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}+xy}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{{x}^{2}+xy}) \\\\  & =(2x+y){e}^{{x}^{2}+xy} \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{{x}^{2}+xy}) \\\\  & =x{e}^{{x}^{2}+xy}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇h(x,y) & =⟨{h}_{x}(x,y),\\,{h}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(2x+y){e}^{{x}^{2}+xy},\\,x{e}^{{x}^{2}+xy}⟩ \\\\ ∇h(1,-1) & =⟨(2(1)+(-1)){e}^{{1}^{2}+(1)(-1)},\\,1\\cdot {e}^{{1}^{2}+(1)(-1)}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124445 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8d807a6-7896-5045-a14a-908b58831340" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points Where a Gradient Vanishes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e2a514c-da70-5cc8-95d2-327d72d03570" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,-2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436987} {:answer/id #uuid "7067d8af-1731-5b47-8f38-e961d32014a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436988} {:answer/id #uuid "8f042e63-ea21-547d-8187-c6464d907f1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(0,0,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436991} {:answer/id #uuid "a6cf6f0f-1cc3-5518-a4d7-359da9629347" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(0,0,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436992} {:answer/id #uuid "fb87d0bd-b556-514b-84e7-e694a530dff2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(-1,0,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437008}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6cf6f0f-1cc3-5518-a4d7-359da9629347" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(0,0,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436992} :answer-field/id #uuid "e83f4999-419d-51c4-a803-c6789f4e1b52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d13fce5d-8bd4-547a-afec-720ff5befaa7" :question/math-academy-id "q-71470" :question/problem "Given $h(x,y,z)=xy{e}^{x}+{z}^{2}{e}^{z}.$ Find the points $(x,y,z)$ where $∇h(x,y,z)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy{e}^{x}+{z}^{2}{e}^{z}) \\\\  & =xy{e}^{x}+y{e}^{x}+0 \\\\  & =y(x+1){e}^{x} \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy{e}^{x}+{z}^{2}{e}^{z}) \\\\  & =x{e}^{x}+0 \\\\  & =x{e}^{x} \\\\ {h}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy{e}^{x}+{z}^{2}{e}^{z}) \\\\  & =0+2z{e}^{z}+{z}^{2}{e}^{z} \\\\  & =z(2+z){e}^{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇h(x,y,z)=y(x+1){e}^{x}\\,i+x{e}^{x}\\,j+z(2+z){e}^{z}\\,k.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇f(x,y,z)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x+1){e}^{x}\\,i+x{e}^{x}\\,j+z(2+z){e}^{z}\\,k & =0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j+0\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$, $j$, and $k$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y(x+1){e}^{x}=0 \\\\ x{e}^{x}=0 \\\\ z(2+z){e}^{z}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second equation gives $x=0.$ Substituting these into the first equation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0+1){e}^{0} & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, from the third equation, we get  $z=0$ or $z=-2.$\n\nTherefore, $∇h(x,y,z)=0$ at two points: $(0,0,0)$ and $(0,0,-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437018 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124447 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67cfec73-130c-5b68-a2e0-5f12837bfc81" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient Using the Properties of the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "caf56146-05fe-53cc-92f2-a535ba4943df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+y)y{e}^{xy}\\text{ }i+x(x+y){e}^{xy}\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436998} {:answer/id #uuid "d2acb1a7-39df-54ef-82c2-34bde846d73e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+y){e}^{x+y}\\text{ }i+(x+y){e}^{x+y}\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436999} {:answer/id #uuid "df8dc6a7-9339-5e43-b61c-4513120ec263" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)y{e}^{x}\\text{ }i+x(1+y){e}^{y}\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437001} {:answer/id #uuid "f072c200-9d5e-5ac5-b478-4233e499425f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)y{e}^{x+y}\\text{ }i+x(1+y){e}^{x+y}\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437005} {:answer/id #uuid "f9e6b982-9c47-56e1-8b69-4a1e0179c1d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy{e}^{xy}\\text{ }i+(x+1)(1+y){e}^{xy}\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437007}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f072c200-9d5e-5ac5-b478-4233e499425f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)y{e}^{x+y}\\text{ }i+x(1+y){e}^{x+y}\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437005} :answer-field/id #uuid "90c23b7b-1640-5aaa-b8b3-64340afb293e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5961ee6e-358e-5baa-837e-dbd941cc54b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-71515" :question/problem "What is the gradient of the product $f(x,y)h(x,y)$ if $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}$ and $h(x,y)=y{e}^{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $h(x,y)$ is given by:\n\n$$\n∇[fh]=f\\,∇\\,h+h\\,∇\\,f\\,\\,\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{y})={e}^{y} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y})=x{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\text{ }j={e}^{y}\\text{ }i+x{e}^{y}\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the partial derivatives of $h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{e}^{x})=y{e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{x})={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇h(x,y) & =\\frac{∂h}{∂x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂h}{∂y}\\text{ }j=y{e}^{x}\\text{ }i+{e}^{x}\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the product rule, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y)h(x,y)] & =x{e}^{y}(y{e}^{x}\\text{ }i+{e}^{x}\\text{ }j)+y{e}^{x}({e}^{y}\\text{ }i+x{e}^{y}\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =xy{e}^{x+y}\\text{ }i+x{e}^{x+y}\\text{ }j+y{e}^{x+y}\\text{ }i+xy{e}^{x+y}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(x+1)y{e}^{x+y}\\text{ }i+x(1+y){e}^{x+y}\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
